 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Tuples, bytes and datetimes round-trip through a tagging layer that sits between the session and the JSON signer.\n\n**Session classes, `src/tessel/sessions.py`:**\n- `SignedCookieSessionInterface` (line 284) is the default session interface. Its `serializer` attribute is `session_json_serializer = TypedCookieSerializer()` (lines 273 and 300). It passes that serializer to the signing serializer it builds (line 316).\n- `SignedCookieSession` (line 57) is the dict-like session object the interface produces.\n\n**Serializer and tags, `src/tessel/json/tag.py`:**\n- `TypedCookieSerializer` (line 219) is the serializer. It holds an ordered list of tag classes and exposes `tag()` for dumping and `untag()` for loading.\n- `TypeMarker` (line 60) is the base class for tags. Each tag has a `key` plus `check()`, `to_json()` and `to_python()` methods.\n- The three tags you asked about are:\n  - `TagTuple` (line 133, key `\" t\"`) stores a tuple as a list.\n  - `TagBytes` (line 159, key `\" b\"`) stores bytes as base64.\n  - `TagDateTime` (line 205, key `\" d\"`) stores a datetime as an HTTP date string.\n- The other tags are `TagDict` (`\" di\"`, escapes dicts that look like tagged values), `PassDict`, `PassList`, `TagMarkup`, and `TagUUID`.\n\n**Flow:**\n1. On save, `tag()` finds the first tag whose `check()` matches. It wraps the value as `{\" t\": [...]}`, `{\" b\": \"...\"}` or `{\" d\": \"...\"}`.\n2. The result is dumped as compact JSON and signed.\n3. On load, `untag()` sees a one-key dict whose key is a registered tag key and calls `to_python()` to restore the original type.\n\nI did not read the method bodies or the line 316 call in detail, only the class and key declarations. The `TagDateTime` \"HTTP date string\" and `TagBytes` \"base64\" details are from the usual design of this serializer, not confirmed in this repo.",
